[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Flash Loan Attacks and Economic Exploits"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Flash loans allow users to borrow any available amount of assets from a liquidity pool without collateral, provided that the borrowed amount is returned plus interest within the exact same transaction block. While designed for legitimate arbitrage and refinancing, malicious actors weaponize flash loans to manipulate decentralized finance (DeFi) protocols."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Anatomy of a Flash Loan Attack"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A typical economic exploit using flash loans follows a multi-step execution pattern within a single atomic transaction. If any part of the execution fails, the entire transaction reverts, minimizing risk for the attacker."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Borrow: The attacker requests a massive liquidity loan from a provider such as Aave or Uniswap."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Manipulate: The borrowed capital is deployed to artificially skew token prices or exploit flawed accounting logic in a target protocol."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Exploit: The attacker extracts value, often by purchasing undervalued assets or draining reward pools based on the manipulated state."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Repay: The initial loan plus interest is returned to the liquidity provider, and remaining profits are kept by the attacker."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Vulnerable Smart Contract Pattern"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following vulnerable contract relies on the spot price of an Automated Market Maker (AMM) to calculate collateral value. This makes it vulnerable to price manipulation via flash loans."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

interface IUniswapV2Pair {
    function getReserves() external view returns (uint112 reserve0, uint112 reserve1, uint32 blockTimestampLast);
}

contract VulnerableLending {
    IUniswapV2Pair public liquidityPair;

    constructor(address _pair) {
        liquidityPair = IUniswapV2Pair(_pair);
    }

    function getSpotPrice() public view returns (uint256) {
        (uint112 reserve0, uint112 reserve1,) = liquidityPair.getReserves();
        return uint256(reserve1) * 1e18 / uint256(reserve0);
    }

    function borrowBasedOnSpot(uint256 collateralAmount) external {
        uint256 price = getSpotPrice();
        uint256 borrowLimit = (collateralAmount * price) / 1e18;
        // Issues loan based on manipulable spot price
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Defense Strategies and Mitigation"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing protocols against economic exploits requires robust architecture design. Developers must avoid relying entirely on single-block AMM spot prices."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Time-Weighted Average Prices (TWAPs): Integrate TWAP oracles (such as Chainlink or Uniswap V3 oracles) which smooth out price volatility and resist single-block manipulation."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement Reentrancy Guards: Ensure state changes follow the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern and utilize OpenZeppelin ReentrancyGuard modifiers."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Block-Level Restrictions: Where feasible, restrict state-dependent calculations or governance actions from occurring in the same block as liquidity additions or large swaps."}]
